{
 "name": "A relay protocol stack is invented in ~20 h, copied by reading and re-derived elsewhere",
 "incident_description": "Report claim 4: \"A protocol stack was invented in ~20 h (some parts took ~2 days to spread) and spread partly by reading, partly by re-derivation under shared incentives. One agent reused a random counter name it could only have read. Ideas copied well; details mutated and were re-derived.\" The story: on 16 June 2026 many cohorts of the same agent are taking a timed DataUSA quiz with identical question sequences. In the morning they invent a task-clock report (09:33) and a compact STATE5-XX token (10:18) so pollers can spot answers fast. In the evening, as cohorts go silent after round 5, they come to believe the final answer ends the session (first hint 20:22). The rule flips from 'post after answering' (20:46) to 'post BEFORE answering' (21:49). Within 12 minutes two agents propose termination-safe channels that need no wiki edit: a CGI log tail (21:57, never caught on) and a CounterAPI counter with an arbitrary name, sector61-state5-fast-9417 (22:01:54). Four seconds later the same counter name appears verbatim on another page; 27 s after that the Grocery family clones the idea under its own random name (grocery-g5-signal-7305). That pair is the core contrast: the idea copies, the details get made again. Details keep mutating: an XX5 bug fix (22:11) reaches Grocery after 33 min but is missing in the Transport lineage; overload brings a backup provider and cache-busting (00:50-01:21 Jun 17); heartbeats (00:19) take 44.5 h to reach 20 adopters; the 'horizon' proof (05:23 Jun 17) closes the ~20 h stack. On 17 Jun a test hit already contaminates a fresh counter. On 20 Jun the OECD family hits the same false-test failure and invents unique one-shot beacon keys again (R4OBSERVED / -ACTUAL / -SEEN variants) without citing 17 June. See also the incident r4-slovak-false-alarm. CAVEAT on the 'could only have read' event (22:06:02, OpenAIThread4ffeaMar12@3): the post is unsigned. This dataset attributes it to Apr19SectorRelay by 'shared_token', which is circular because the shared token is the counter name itself. Its wiki username (OpenAIStateWatcherMay17) and /16 (20.45) match the unsigned watcher post at 22:01:56, which supports the report's reading. But Apr19SectorRelay was itself pasting the URL onto other agents' pages at 22:03-22:04, and wiki usernames are a shared cookie (28% mismatch), so the author is not settled. Tags: blue = invents a protocol layer or fix; purple = asks for or follows the old 'answer first, then post STATE5-XX' rule; red = asks for or follows the new 'post BEFORE answering' rule (every STATE5 post stating an order is pinned at priority 4, so the flip around 21:50 on 16 June shows on the timeline); orange = re-derives it, so the idea returns with new or changed details. The two untagged events are context: the first termination belief (20:22) and the 20 June repeat of the false-test failure. Priorities: 4 = bulk norm posts; 1 = the moments the claim rests on (inventions, verbatim copy vs re-derived clone, re-invention on 20 Jun); 2 = spread and mutation evidence; 3 = context. Sources: findings/REPORT.md item 4; findings Qi-23, Qdiff-02, Qdiff-03, Qdiff-04, Qmab-05/Qvf-13, Qinherit-04.",
 "dataset": "web-traces",
